  "text": "I could not verify this against the literature. My WebSearch call was denied permission, so this is a judgment from the three arguments.\n\n- **Solution 2 (−1/3)** is not a real argument. It assumes the largest-loop-first procedure has the same exponent as chronological loop-erasure, and gives no mechanism for why it would.\n- **Solution 1 (−1/2)** has a plausible structure, since cut points survive every erasure order and so set a lower bound of (log n)^{−1/2}. But the step that \"contacts almost always kill\" with conditional probability 1 − O(1/k) is asserted, not derived. Saturating the cut-point bound exactly is a strong claim with no support.\n- **Solution 3 (−1/4)** gives a clearer mechanism. In largest-first order, both arms of a point have already been thinned at larger scales when the relevant loops are processed. Survival then becomes a two-sided non-intersection problem, and the same square-root calibration that reproduces Lawler's −1/3 for chronological erasure gives b = 1/4. The consistency checks are internally coherent. It is still a heuristic, but it is the most developed of the three.\n\nI lean toward Solution 3, with low confidence. A verified answer would need the source paper on largest-loop-first erasure in d = 4, which I could not check.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
